WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.440
Part 3. You will hear two sociology students called Tamara and Dev discussing their research

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:18.400
for a presentation on loneliness. First, you have some time to look at questions 21-26.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Now listen carefully and answer questions 21-26.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:02.000
Shall we go through the notes we've made from our research into loneliness now, Dev?

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:12.000
OK, Tamara. It's been a real eye-opener. I had no idea that loneliness has been increasing steadily for the last 20 years.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:19.000
I know. And it's the same all over the world. The downside of a modern lifestyle, I guess.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Did you come to any conclusions about what the reasons for the increase are?

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Well, I'd assumed it was mainly an issue for the elderly, but in fact it's something which affects young people just as much.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:47.000
So nothing really to do with longer lifespans. What about social media? In my case, far from making me feel isolated, it actually does the opposite.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:55.000
It definitely does more good than harm. I'd say loneliness has a lot to do with the way cities are designed.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:00.000
People living in high-rise flats with not much opportunity to speak to their neighbours.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.000
I think you're right.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Another possible reason is that people are having fewer children and don't live in large extended family groups.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:18.000
But in this country, anyway, that all changed decades ago. And yet loneliness is a more recent problem.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:30.000
I suppose so. A more plausible explanation is that people are having to move around for work and often end up living miles away from their family and friends.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.000
That's true.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:41.000
Looking at the studies on health risks and loneliness, there are claims that loneliness has as much impact as smoking 15 cigarettes a day.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:49.000
Or similar to the risks caused by obesity. But I'm not sure there's enough evidence for some of these claims.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:58.000
Well, what about that one in Finland, which showed that loneliness increased the risk of cancer by about 10%?

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.000
And those findings have been supported by other studies too.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:08.000
You're right about that one. I was actually thinking of the studies on dementia.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Some found no association between loneliness and dementia and others found the opposite.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:25.000
Not exactly reliable then. There's been a lot of research on cardiovascular disease and whether loneliness contributes to that.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:37.000
Yes, I read that it was hard to reach a judgement as the definition of loneliness varied quite a lot and the responses from participants were too subjective.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:43.000
But there's no doubt that loneliness contributes to a weakened immune system.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:47.000
Unquestionably. The data on that is sound.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:51.000
What did you think about the evolutionary theory of loneliness?

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:59.000
Well, I thought the idea that loneliness evolved because it motivated people to be with other people quite convincing.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:03.000
Survival often depended on group cooperation.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:15.000
But I don't think there's enough evidence to claim that there must be a group of neurons in our brains which influence social behaviour by making us feel bad when we're alone.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:23.000
There are a few studies which support the theory, but not conclusively enough. More evidence is needed.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:30.000
And anyway, this theory's not really useful when it comes to solving the problem of loneliness today.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.000
True.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:42.000
Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 27 to 30.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:16.000
Now listen and answer questions 27 to 30.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:24.000
Should we look at the relationship between loneliness and mental health now?

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:34.000
OK. So loneliness and depression are clearly related, and that's been recognised by various governments around the world.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:40.000
But unlike depression, loneliness has no recognised clinical form.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:46.000
There's no available diagnosis or effective treatment, and that's not likely to change.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.000
I don't think so either.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:55.000
I was thinking we should start our presentation with an example from our own experience.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:03.000
I'd like to talk about how lonely I was when I started university, being away from home for the first time and all that.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:12.000
Good idea. Everyone will be able to relate to that, although a lot of students were probably too embarrassed to admit to it.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:20.000
Yeah. We could discuss ways of dealing with loneliness as well, like just talking to strangers.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:30.000
Loads of studies have shown that interactions with shop assistants and bar staff make people feel more optimistic and relaxed.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:36.000
I don't know about that, but it must make people feel more connected with their community.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:43.000
True, although you need to be a certain kind of person to be able to just strike up a conversation.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:53.000
Good point. We should say something about solitude and how being alone and being lonely aren't the same thing.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:59.000
It's strange the way some people can't stand being by themselves while others love it.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:06.000
Yeah, the research shows a certain amount of solitude is beneficial for well-being, which I appreciate,

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:14.000
but being alone isn't something I actually like. I'd never choose to go on holiday alone, for example.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.000
Me neither.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.000
Well, let's not...

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:29.000
That is the end of Part 3. You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to Part 3.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:51.000
Thank you for watching!

